Rem ==================================================================
Rem fin.shoddy — test program for machines/fin.shoddy
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the csvtest.shoddy / jsontest.shoddy pattern: it
Rem prints what it is doing, stops at the first failed assertion, and
Rem ends with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when none fail.
Rem
Rem Every number here is a KNOWN ANSWER — a textbook figure, a
Rem spreadsheet's, or one derived by hand — never a value recorded from
Rem a run. A finance library whose tests record its own output tests
Rem nothing at all, and is exactly the kind that gets trusted anyway.
Rem
Rem The two that matter most are easy to miss. FinComb(52, 5) must be
Rem 2598960 EXACTLY: the factorial form overflows past 2^53 before it
Rem gets there, so an exact answer proves the multiplicative form is
Rem what ran. And FinDivGrowth is checked on a series that falls and
Rem recovers, where a geometric average and an arithmetic one disagree —
Rem on a smooth series they agree and the test proves nothing.
Rem
Rem What cannot be tested here: the aborting half of each guard. Shoddy
Rem has no catchable errors, so FinIrr's refusal on a rootless series,
Rem FinYtm's on an unpriceable bond, FinNper's on a payment that never
Rem clears the interest and FinBreakEvenOf's on a negative contribution
Rem margin are all verified by hand and named here instead.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/fin.shoddy"
Include "../machines/julian.shoddy"

Rem Money and rates carry more places than they print, so compare to a
Rem tolerance. The answers that must be exact are asserted with = and
Rem would otherwise hide a real defect behind a tolerance.
Def Near(a As Number, b As Number, tol As Number) As Boolean
    Abs(a - b) <= tol

Def Main()
    Print("--- RATES AND CONVERSION ---")
    Assert(Near(FinPct(5), 0.05, 0.0000001), "5 PERCENT IS 0.05")
    Assert(Near(FinAsPct(0.05), 5, 0.0000001), "AND BACK AGAIN")
    Rem 12% nominal compounded monthly is 12.6825% effective.
    Assert(Near(FinEff(0.12, 12), 0.1268250, 0.0000005), "NOMINAL TO EFFECTIVE")
    Assert(Near(FinNom(FinEff(0.12, 12), 12), 0.12, 0.0000001), "AND BACK AGAIN")
    Assert(Near(FinRule72(0.08), 9, 0.0000001), "RULE OF 72 AT 8 PERCENT")
    Rem Fisher exact, not 10 - 4: (1.10 / 1.04) - 1 = 5.769%.
    Assert(Near(FinReal(0.10, 0.04), 0.0576923, 0.0000005), "REAL RETURN IS FISHER, NOT SUBTRACTION")
    Assert(Not Near(FinReal(0.10, 0.04), 0.06, 0.0005), "AND IS NOT THE APPROXIMATION")
    Assert(Near(FinPctChg(80, 100), 0.25, 0.0000001), "PERCENT CHANGE")
    Assert(Near(FinToBps(0.0125), 125, 0.0000001), "BASIS POINTS OUT")
    Assert(Near(FinFromBps(125), 0.0125, 0.0000001), "AND BACK")

    Print("--- TIME VALUE OF MONEY ---")
    Assert(Near(FinFv(1000, 0.05, 10), 1628.894627, 0.000001), "FUTURE VALUE")
    Assert(Near(FinPv(FinFv(1000, 0.05, 10), 0.05, 10), 1000, 0.000001), "PRESENT VALUE INVERTS IT")
    Rem The canonical 30-year mortgage: 200000 at 6% APR, monthly.
    Assert(Near(FinPmt(200000, 0, 0.005, 360), 1199.101, 0.001), "THE 30-YEAR MORTGAGE PAYMENT")
    Assert(Near(FinPmtDue(200000, 0, 0.005, 360),
                FinPmt(200000, 0, 0.005, 360) / 1.005, 0.000001), "PAID AT THE START OF THE PERIOD")
    Assert(Near(FinPvAnn(100, 0.05, 10), 772.173493, 0.000001), "PRESENT VALUE OF AN ANNUITY")
    Assert(Near(FinFvAnn(100, 0.05, 10), 1257.789254, 0.000001), "FUTURE VALUE OF AN ANNUITY")
    Assert(Near(FinPvAnnDue(100, 0.05, 10), FinPvAnn(100, 0.05, 10) * 1.05, 0.000001), "ANNUITY DUE")
    Assert(Near(FinNper(200000, 0, 1199.101, 0.005), 360, 0.01), "AND HOW MANY PAYMENTS THAT IS")

    Print("--- ZERO RATE ---")
    Rem A 0% promotional loan is a real input, not an edge case: every
    Rem word that divides by rate has to answer at the limit.
    Assert(Near(FinPmt(1200, 0, 0, 12), 100, 0.0000001), "PAYMENT AT ZERO RATE")
    Assert(Near(FinPvAnn(100, 0, 12), 1200, 0.0000001), "ANNUITY PV AT ZERO RATE")
    Assert(Near(FinFvAnn(100, 0, 12), 1200, 0.0000001), "ANNUITY FV AT ZERO RATE")
    Assert(Near(FinNper(1200, 0, 100, 0), 12, 0.0000001), "PERIODS AT ZERO RATE")

    Print("--- INTEREST ---")
    Assert(Near(Total(FinSimple(1000, 0.05, 3)), 1150, 0.0000001), "SIMPLE INTEREST TOTAL")
    Assert(Near(Interest(FinSimple(1000, 0.05, 3)), 150, 0.0000001), "AND ITS INTEREST")
    Assert(Near(Total(FinCompound(1000, 0.05, 1, 3)), 1157.625, 0.0000001), "COMPOUND INTEREST TOTAL")

    Print("--- LOANS ---")
    Rem The whole point of the closed forms: a schedule that disagrees
    Rem with the balance is a schedule nobody can reconcile.
    Assert(Near(Balance(FinAmortAt(200000, 0.005, 1199.101, 360)), 0, 0.5), "THE MORTGAGE CLOSES")
    Assert(Near(FinSum(Map(FinSchedule(200000, 0.005, 360, 1199.101), Principal)),
                200000, 0.5), "AND ITS PRINCIPAL COLUMN SUMS TO THE LOAN")
    Assert(Near(Interest(FinAmortAt(200000, 0.005, 1199.101, 1)), 1000, 0.0000001),
           "FIRST MONTH IS ALMOST ALL INTEREST")
    Assert(Near(Principal(FinAmortRange(200000, 0.005, 1199.101, 1, 12))
                + Balance(FinAmortRange(200000, 0.005, 1199.101, 1, 12)), 200000, 0.5),
           "A RANGE AGGREGATES TO THE SAME PLACE")
    Assert(Periods(FinExtraPayoff(200000, 0.005, 1199.101, 200)) > 0, "EXTRA PAYMENTS SAVE TIME")
    Assert(Interest(FinExtraPayoff(200000, 0.005, 1199.101, 200)) > 0, "AND MONEY")
    Assert(Periods(FinCardPayoff(2000, 0.18, 100)) > 0, "A CARD AT A FIXED PAYMENT CLEARS")
    Assert(Periods(FinCardMinPayoff(2000, 0.18, 0.02))
           > Periods(FinCardPayoff(2000, 0.18, 100)), "AND THE MINIMUM TAKES FAR LONGER")

    Print("--- INVESTMENT APPRAISAL ---")
    Assert(Near(FinIrr({ 0 - 1000, 400, 400, 400 }), 0.09701, 0.00001), "IRR OF AN EVEN SERIES")
    Rem The property that ties the two NPV conventions together.
    Assert(Near(FinNpvOf({ 0 - 1000, 400, 400, 400 }, FinIrr({ 0 - 1000, 400, 400, 400 })),
                0, 0.0001), "NPV AT THE IRR IS ZERO")
    Assert(Near(FinNpv(1000, { 400, 400, 400 }, 0.09701), 0, 0.001), "AND THE OTHER CONVENTION AGREES")
    Assert(Near(FinPayback(1000, { 400, 400, 400 }), 2.5, 0.0000001), "PAYBACK INTERPOLATES")
    Assert(Near(FinPayback(1000, { 100, 100 }), 0 - 1, 0.0000001), "AND REFUSES WHEN IT NEVER RECOVERS")
    Assert(FinPaybackDisc(1000, { 400, 400, 400 }, 0.05) > FinPayback(1000, { 400, 400, 400 }),
           "DISCOUNTING MAKES PAYBACK LONGER")
    Assert(Near(FinCagr(100, 121, 2), 0.1, 0.0000001), "CAGR")
    Assert(Near(FinRoi(100, 150), 0.5, 0.0000001), "ROI")
    Assert(Near(FinNfv({ 100, 100 }, 0.1), 210, 0.0000001), "NET FUTURE VALUE")
    Assert(Length(FinExpand({ FinFlow(50, 3), FinFlow(75, 2) })) = 5, "A CASH FLOW LIST EXPANDS")
    Assert(Near(FinSum(FinExpand({ FinFlow(50, 3), FinFlow(75, 2) })), 300, 0.0000001),
           "TO THE RIGHT TOTAL")
    Assert(FinMirr({ 0 - 1000, 400, 400, 400 }, 0.05, 0.05) > 0, "MIRR IS POSITIVE ON A PROFITABLE SERIES")

    Print("--- STATISTICS, WRAPPED ---")
    Rem Sample and population differ visibly at n = 4. Getting these two
    Rem swapped is invisible until someone checks against a spreadsheet.
    Assert(Near(FinStdDev({ 2, 4, 4, 6 }), 1.632993, 0.000001), "SAMPLE STANDARD DEVIATION (N-1)")
    Assert(Near(FinStdDevP({ 2, 4, 4, 6 }), 1.414214, 0.000001), "POPULATION STANDARD DEVIATION (N)")
    Assert(Near(FinVar({ 2, 4, 4, 6 }), 2.666667, 0.000001), "SAMPLE VARIANCE")
    Assert(Near(FinVarP({ 2, 4, 4, 6 }), 2, 0.0000001), "POPULATION VARIANCE")
    Assert(Near(Avg(FinStat1({ 2, 4, 4, 6 })), 4, 0.0000001), "THE SUMMARY'S MEAN")
    Assert(Near(Sd(FinStat1({ 2, 4, 4, 6 })), FinStdDev({ 2, 4, 4, 6 }), 0.0000001),
           "ITS SD IS THE SAMPLE FORM")
    Assert(Near(Total(FinStat1({ 2, 4, 4, 6 })), 16, 0.0000001), "AND ITS TOTAL IS THE SUM")
    Assert(Near(FinMedian({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }), 2.5, 0.0000001), "MEDIAN")
    Assert(Near(FinQuantile({ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, 0.5), 2.5, 0.0000001), "QUANTILE TAKES A FRACTION")
    Assert(Near(FinMinimum({ 3, 1, 2 }), 1, 0.0000001), "MINIMUM")
    Assert(Near(FinMaximum({ 3, 1, 2 }), 3, 0.0000001), "MAXIMUM")
    Assert(Near(FinRangeOf({ 3, 1, 2 }), 2, 0.0000001), "RANGE")
    Assert(Near(FinWeightedMean({ 1, 2 }, { 1, 3 }), 1.75, 0.0000001), "WEIGHTED MEAN")
    Assert(Near(FinCorrel({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 4, 6 }), 1, 0.0000001), "PERFECT CORRELATION")
    Assert(Near(FinNormP(0, 0, 1), 0.5, 0.0000001), "THE NORMAL AT ITS MEAN")
    Assert(Near(FinRound(1.2345, 2), 1.23, 0.0000001), "ROUNDING TO PLACES")

    Print("--- GROWTH AVERAGES GEOMETRICALLY ---")
    Assert(Near(FinDivGrowth({ 100, 110, 121 }), 0.1, 0.000001), "A SMOOTH SERIES GROWS AT 10 PERCENT")
    Rem This is the one that proves it. A series that falls and recovers
    Rem has a geometric growth of 10% and an arithmetic one of 15.5%.
    Assert(Near(FinDivGrowth({ 100, 90, 121 }), 0.1, 0.000001),
           "AND SO DOES ONE THAT FALLS AND RECOVERS")
    Assert(Not Near(FinDivGrowth({ 100, 90, 121 }), 0.155, 0.01),
           "WHICH AN ARITHMETIC MEAN WOULD GET WRONG")
    Assert(Near(AvgReturn(FinRStat({ 0.1, 0.2, 0.3 })), 0.2, 0.0000001), "RETURN STATISTICS")
    Assert(Near(FinYield(2.50, 50), 0.05, 0.0000001), "DIVIDEND YIELD")
    Assert(Dca(FinDcaOf(1200, 3, { 10, 5, 10 })) > Lump(FinDcaOf(1200, 3, { 10, 5, 10 })),
           "AVERAGING IN BEATS THE LUMP SUM WHEN THE PRICE DIPS")

    Print("--- REGRESSION ---")
    Assert(Near(Slope(FinFitOf({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 4, 6 }, FinLinear())), 2, 0.000001),
           "A LINE THROUGH THE ORIGIN")
    Assert(Near(Intercept(FinFitOf({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 4, 6 }, FinLinear())), 0, 0.000001),
           "HAS NO INTERCEPT")
    Assert(Near(RFit(FinFitOf({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 4, 6 }, FinLinear())), 1, 0.000001),
           "AND FITS PERFECTLY")
    Assert(Near(Slope(FinFitOf({ 1, 2, 3 }, { 2, 4, 8 }, FinExpModel())), 0.693147, 0.000001),
           "AN EXPONENTIAL DOUBLES EVERY STEP")

    Print("--- SMALL BUSINESS ---")
    Rem Cost 60 sold at 100: 40% margin, 66.7% markup. The same trade,
    Rem two numbers, and the most common confusion in the group.
    Assert(Near(FinMarginOf(60, 100), 0.4, 0.0000001), "MARGIN IS ON THE SELLING PRICE")
    Assert(Near(FinMarkupOf(60, 100), 0.666667, 0.000001), "MARKUP IS ON THE COST")
    Assert(Near(FinSellFromMargin(60, 0.4), 100, 0.0000001), "AND EACH SOLVER INVERTS ITS PARTNER")
    Assert(Near(FinCostFromMargin(100, 0.4), 60, 0.0000001), "BOTH WAYS")
    Assert(Near(Units(FinBreakEvenOf(1000, 5, 15, 0)), 100, 0.0000001), "BREAK-EVEN UNITS")
    Assert(Near(Margin(FinBreakEvenOf(1000, 5, 15, 0)), 10, 0.0000001), "AND THE CONTRIBUTION MARGIN")
    Assert(Near(Units(FinBreakEvenOf(1000, 5, 15, 500)), 150, 0.0000001), "A PROFIT TARGET RAISES IT")
    Assert(Near(Net(FinPayrollOf(1000, { 0.2, 0.05 })), 750, 0.0000001), "GROSS TO NET")
    Assert(Near(Withheld(FinPayrollOf(1000, { 0.2, 0.05 })), 250, 0.0000001), "AND WHAT WAS WITHHELD")
    Assert(Near(Total(FinLateOf(1000, 0.12, 30, Fin360())), 1010, 0.0000001), "LATE INTEREST ON 30/360")
    Assert(Near(Nth(FinCashProj(100, { 50, 50 }, { 20, 20 }), 2), 160, 0.0000001), "CASH PROJECTION RUNS")
    Assert(Near(FinNetWorth({ 100, 200 }, { 50 }), 250, 0.0000001), "NET WORTH")
    Assert(Near(SavingsRate(FinBudgetOf({ 1000 }, { 800 })), 0.2, 0.0000001), "SAVINGS RATE")
    Assert(Near(SavingsRate(FinBudgetOf({ 0 }, { 0 })), 0, 0.0000001), "AND IS ZERO, NOT NAN, ON NO INCOME")

    Print("--- GOALS AND PROJECTIONS ---")
    Assert(Near(FinGoalPmt(1257.789254, 0, 0.05, 10), 100, 0.000001), "WHAT MUST I PUT BY")
    Assert(Near(FinRetire(0, 100, 12, 0.12, 1), FinFvAnn(100, 0.01, 12), 0.000001),
           "AND WHAT IT COMES TO")

    Print("--- DEPRECIATION ---")
    Rem Cost 10000, salvage 1000, life 5.
    Assert(Near(FinSl(10000, 1000, 5), 1800, 0.0000001), "STRAIGHT LINE")
    Assert(Near(Nth(FinSlBook(10000, 1000, 5), 5), 1000, 0.0000001), "DOWN TO THE SALVAGE FLOOR")
    Assert(Near(Amount(FinSyd(10000, 1000, 5, 1)), 3000, 0.0000001), "SUM OF YEARS DIGITS, YEAR 1")
    Assert(Near(Amount(FinSyd(10000, 1000, 5, 5)), 600, 0.0000001), "AND YEAR 5")
    Assert(Near(Amount(FinDb(10000, 1000, 5, 2, 1)), 4000, 0.0000001), "DOUBLE DECLINING, YEAR 1")
    Rem With salvage 1000 the declining charge never falls below the
    Rem straight-line one, so no crossover happens and the schedule is
    Rem declining balance throughout, the last year clipped by the floor.
    Assert(Method(FinDbx(10000, 1000, 5, 2, 1)) = FinDbUsed(), "DECLINING BALANCE LEADS")
    Assert(Method(FinDbx(10000, 1000, 5, 2, 5)) = FinDbUsed(), "AND HOLDS WHEN SALVAGE IS HIGH")
    Assert(Near(FinSum(Map(Range(1, 5), Fn(k) => Amount(FinDbx(10000, 1000, 5, 2, k)))),
                9000, 0.0000001), "AND ITS SCHEDULE SUMS TO COST LESS SALVAGE")
    Rem Drop the floor and the crossover appears in year 4, where
    Rem straight-line on the remaining two years beats 40% of the book.
    Assert(Method(FinDbx(10000, 0, 5, 2, 3)) = FinDbUsed(), "WITH NO SALVAGE IT STILL LEADS")
    Assert(Method(FinDbx(10000, 0, 5, 2, 4)) = FinSlUsed(), "UNTIL STRAIGHT LINE OVERTAKES IT")
    Assert(Method(FinDbx(10000, 0, 5, 2, 5)) = FinSlUsed(), "AND KEEPS IT TO THE END")
    Assert(Near(FinSum(Map(Range(1, 5), Fn(k) => Amount(FinDbx(10000, 0, 5, 2, k)))),
                10000, 0.0000001), "WRITING THE WHOLE ASSET OFF")

    Print("--- COMBINATORICS ---")
    Assert(Near(FinFact(5), 120, 0.0000001), "FACTORIAL")
    Assert(Near(FinPerm(10, 3), 720, 0.0000001), "PERMUTATIONS")
    Rem Exactly, not nearly: the factorial form overflows past 2^53 long
    Rem before it reaches this, so an exact answer proves the
    Rem multiplicative form is what ran.
    Assert(FinComb(52, 5) = 2598960, "A POKER HAND, EXACTLY")
    Assert(FinComb(5, 2) = 10, "AND A SMALL ONE")

    Print("--- DAY COUNTS ---")
    Rem The CALENDAR's own assertions — leap rules, serial round trips,
    Rem month clamping — moved to tst/julian.shoddy with the arithmetic,
    Rem on the same fixtures. What is asserted here is what stayed in
    Rem fin: the BASES, which are finance's fiction about the calendar.
    Rem The NASD adjustment case: D1 is 31 so it becomes 30, and the
    Rem count from there to 28 February is 28 days.
    Assert(FinDays360(JulOf(2026, 1, 31), JulOf(2026, 2, 28)) = 28, "THE 30/360 END-OF-MONTH RULE")
    Assert(FinDays360(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2027, 1, 1)) = 360, "AND A WHOLE 30/360 YEAR")
    Assert(FinDateDiff(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2027, 1, 1), FinActual()) = 365,
           "AN ACTUAL YEAR IS 365 DAYS")
    Assert(Near(FinYearFrac(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2027, 1, 1), Fin360()), 1, 0.0000001),
           "AND A 30/360 YEAR IS ONE")

    Print("--- BONDS ---")
    Rem A bond priced at par yields its coupon: the single property that
    Rem checks price and yield against each other.
    Assert(Near(FinBondPrice(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2031, 1, 1),
                             0.06, 0.06, FinSemi(), Fin360()), 100, 0.0001),
           "A 6 PERCENT BOND AT A 6 PERCENT YIELD PRICES AT PAR")
    Assert(Near(FinYtm(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2031, 1, 1),
                       0.06, 100, FinSemi(), Fin360()), 0.06, 0.000001),
           "AND YIELDS 6 PERCENT AT PAR")
    Assert(FinBondPrice(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2031, 1, 1), 0.06, 0.08, FinSemi(), Fin360())
           < 100, "A HIGHER YIELD PRICES BELOW PAR")
    Assert(FinBondPrice(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2031, 1, 1), 0.06, 0.04, FinSemi(), Fin360())
           > 100, "AND A LOWER ONE ABOVE IT")
    Assert(Near(FinAccrued(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2026, 1, 1),
                           0.06, FinSemi(), Fin360()), 0, 0.0000001),
           "NOTHING HAS ACCRUED ON A COUPON DATE")
    Assert(Near(FinAccrued(JulOf(2026, 4, 1), JulOf(2026, 1, 1),
                           0.06, FinSemi(), Fin360()), 1.5, 0.0001),
           "AND HALF A PERIOD IS HALF A COUPON")
    Assert(FinMdur(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2031, 1, 1), 0.06, 0.06, FinSemi()) > 0,
           "MODIFIED DURATION IS POSITIVE")
    Assert(FinMdur(JulOf(2026, 1, 1), JulOf(2031, 1, 1), 0.06, 0.06, FinSemi()) < 5,
           "AND SHORTER THAN THE BOND")

    Print("--- PRESENTATION ---")
    Assert(FinFmt(1234.5) = "1,234.50", "GROUPED THOUSANDS AND TWO PLACES")
    Assert(FinFmt(0) = "0.00", "ZERO")
    Assert(FinFmt(0 - 1234.5) = "-1,234.50", "AND A NEGATIVE KEEPS ITS SIGN")
    Assert(FinFmt(999999.99) = "999,999.99", "A LARGE ONE GROUPS CORRECTLY")

    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")
